## Repository map

- `config/`: global Django settings, URL wiring, WSGI/ASGI.
- `apps/`: domain apps and shared platform code.
- `apps/core/`: the most important shared app; already partially refactored into layered structure.
- `templates/`: shared templates.
- `static/`: source assets.
- `media/`: local uploads and generated artifacts.
- `docs/`: technical documentation and refactoring guidance.

Main apps:

- `accounts`, `iam`, `workshops`, `collaborators`: account, auth, roles, workshop context.
- `budget`, `workorder`, `scheduling`: operational workflow.
- `catalog`, `stock`, `suppliers`, `customer`, `checklist`, `quote`: service/product/inspection ecosystem.
- `finance`: finance, DRE, tax emission, Webmania, webhook flows.
- `messaging`: communication templates and customer message groups.
- `core`: shared contracts, providers, middleware, forms, tables, filtering, search, PDF, integration abstractions.

## Current architecture reality

This repository is in transition.

- It is still a Django monolith.
- Not every app follows the target layered architecture yet.
- `apps/core/` already reflects the new direction better than most apps.
- Some business apps are still flat or semi-flat, with logic in models, services, and views.

Practical rule:

- Follow existing working patterns in the touched app.
- But when introducing new code, prefer the direction defined in `docs/REFACTORING_GUIDE.md`.
- Do not do broad rewrites unless the task explicitly asks for refactoring.
